 the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={y}_{p}(2{x}_{\\text{new}}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $3$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+1=2\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈2.$" 13194139535296 true] [17592186342348 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342349 13194139535296 true] [17592186342348 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342350 13194139535296 true] [17592186342348 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535296 true] [17592186342348 1041 "field-1" 13194139535296 true] [17592186342348 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342349 13194139535296 true] [17592186342348 1110 #uuid "a8cfd1f6-cb49-59c2-abc7-c25aa75b6b1d" 13194139535296 true] [17592186342349 1034 "2" 13194139535296 true] [17592186342349 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535296 true] [17592186342349 1111 #uuid "89adb6e8-2370-5626-9b56-83883b9dd5c4" 13194139535296 true] [17592186342350 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535296 true] [17592186342350 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535296 true] [17592186342350 1111 #uuid "9f90065e-a0ff-59a1-99e2-2137fa670815" 13194139535296 true] [17592186342351 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342352 13194139535296 true] [17592186342351 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342354 13194139535296 true] [17592186342351 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342356 13194139535296 true] [17592186342351 1089 "q-331560" 13194139535296 true] [17592186342351 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535296 true] [17592186342351 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" 13194139535296 true] [17592186342351 11